```js
const row = await db.write(
  "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
  [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
);
if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
res.json(row);
```

**`routes/accounts.js:6` and `:14`**: a user can only ever act on their own account, so check the path id against the session in SQL. Doing it in SQL means Postgres handles the string-versus-number comparison:

```js
"UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
[req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
```

```js
const row = await db.write(
  "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
  [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
);
if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
res.status(204).end();
```

The 404 check matters. `db.write` returns `res.rows[0]`, which is `undefined` when nothing matches. Without the check, a blocked cross-account request still gets a 200 with an empty body, or a 204 on delete, so it looks like it succeeded.

One question the code doesn't answer: should every user in an account be able to rename or soft-delete it? `req.user` has no role, so this fix stops writes across accounts but not a regular member deleting their own account.

For defense in depth, row-level security in Postgres could sit on top of this. `write()` is the only place a transaction begins, so it could run `SET LOCAL app.account_id` there. That would mean passing the account id into `db.write` and `db.query`.

Separately, `auth.js:8` reads `req.cookies`, but `app.js` never mounts `cookie-parser`. As written, every request gets a 401, so you'll need to fix that before you can test any of this.

