DELETE needs the same `AND id = $2` and the `!row` check. Right now it returns 204 even when nothing was updated.

Use 404 rather than 403, so an id from another account looks the same as one that doesn't exist. If you'd rather enforce this in one place than in every query, the next step is Postgres row-level security, set with `SET LOCAL` inside `write()`'s transaction.

This leaves one open question. Users only have `id`, `email` and `account_id`, so after the fix any user on an account can still rename it or soft-delete it. Should DELETE be limited to an owner or admin role, and is there a role anywhere to check?

Separately, `app.js` never installs `cookie-parser`, so `req.cookies` is undefined. As written, every request gets a 401 in `sessionUser`, which will get in the way when you test this change.

I'd add one middleware in `app.js` just above `app.use(sessionUser)` at line 11. At that position it also logs the requests that `sessionUser` rejects with 401. It writes the log line when the response closes, and by then `req.user` has been set if the request got past authentication:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
      completed: res.writableFinished,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

It listens for `"close"` instead of `"finish"` so that requests the client gives up on are still logged. Those show up with `completed: false`, which covers the hung-write case from before. Leave request bodies and cookies out of the log: the bodies contain `billing_email`, and the cookie is the session token.

The decision this depends on is where the logs go. Plain JSON on stdout is enough if whatever runs the process collects it. If you already use a log stack that expects a particular format or a particular logger such as pino, match that instead. Which is it?

